Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?dos:=20Phase=2011=20patch=200036=20=E2=80=94=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?replace-not-chain=20IRQ-1=20keyboard=20hook?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Bug: real-HW operator on Gateway 2000 Pentium-OD-83 + AMI/Award BIOS +
Vibra16S reports keyboard input works for ~10 keystrokes, then PC
speaker beeps and further keys are dropped. Held-key navigation still
works; single-press timing is broken.

Root cause: DOSVESA_InitKeyboard hooks IRQ-1 via DOS_HookInterrupt,
which uses _go32_dpmi_chain_protected_mode_interrupt_vector. The chain
wrapper runs our KeyboardIRQHandler first, then falls through to
CWSDPMI's reflection-to-real-mode stub, which forwards the IRQ to the
real-mode BIOS INT 9 handler. BIOS INT 9 writes the keystroke into the
BIOS keyboard buffer at 0040:001E in parallel with our SDL ring. The
application never calls INT 16h to drain that buffer, so it fills
after ~10-16 keystrokes and BIOS beeps the PC speaker on overflow.
AMI/Award BIOSes also commonly issue an 8042 "disable keyboard"
controller command on buffer-full, which stops further IRQ-1 fires
until BIOS re-enables on the (never-coming) INT 16h drain -- that's the
"input stops responding" portion of the symptom.

Fix: add DOS_HookInterruptReplace, a variant of DOS_HookInterrupt that
uses _go32_dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector (replace) instead
of _go32_dpmi_chain_protected_mode_interrupt_vector (chain). The
replace variant allocates an explicit IRET wrapper via
_go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper since the plain "set" function does
not internally wrap raw C handlers with register-save / IRET glue
(unlike the chain function which does).

DOSVESA_InitKeyboard switches to DOS_HookInterruptReplace for IRQ-1.
Audio (SB16, IRQ-5) and any other future hook keep using the chaining
DOS_HookInterrupt -- only IRQ-1 has a BIOS handler with stateful side
effects in its chain.

DOS_InterruptHook gains a `replaced` flag so DOS_UnhookInterrupt can
distinguish the two paths and call _go32_dpmi_free_iret_wrapper safely
on the replace path (the chain wrapper still cannot be freed; that
leak is harmless and pre-existing).

Risk audit: engine consumes raw scancodes via SDL's keymap, never
reads BIOS keyboard state (shift flags at 0040:0017, scan-to-ASCII
translation, the 0040:001E buffer). Ctrl+Alt+Del system reset becomes
unreachable mid-session -- acceptable for a port that owns the
keyboard.

No new env var. Default-behavior fix.

See docs/PHASE11-KEYBOARD-FINDINGS.md for the full mechanism analysis,
hypothesis triage (a refuted, b confirmed, c subsumed), and
pressure-test against all five observed real-HW behaviors.
---
 src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c        | 84 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h        | 25 ++++++++++-
 src/video/dos/SDL_dosevents.c | 17 ++++++-
 3 files changed, 120 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
index 0ba077319..cf260a48a 100644
--- a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
+++ b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
@@ -95,6 +95,7 @@ void DOS_HookInterrupt(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook)
     hook->fn = fn;
     hook->irq = irq;
     hook->interrupt_vector = DOS_IRQToVector(irq);
+    hook->replaced = false; // chain variant; see DOS_UnhookInterrupt for why this matters
     hook->irq_handler_seginfo.pm_selector = _go32_my_cs();
     hook->irq_handler_seginfo.pm_offset = (uint32_t)fn;
     _go32_dpmi_get_protected_mode_interrupt_vector(hook->interrupt_vector, &hook->original_irq_handler_seginfo);
@@ -109,6 +110,73 @@ void DOS_HookInterrupt(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook)
     }
 }
 
+/* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): replace-not-chain variant. Used by
+   DOSVESA_InitKeyboard for IRQ-1 only. The chain variant
+   DOS_HookInterrupt runs our handler FIRST, then falls through to the
+   previous handler at the protected-mode IRQ-1 vector -- which is
+   CWSDPMI's reflection-to-real-mode stub, which forwards to the
+   real-mode BIOS INT 9 handler. BIOS INT 9 then writes the keystroke
+   into the BIOS keyboard buffer at 0040:001E. Since the application
+   never calls INT 16h to drain that buffer, it fills after ~10-16
+   keystrokes and BIOS issues a PC speaker beep on overflow; AMI/Award
+   BIOSes commonly also issue an 8042 "disable keyboard" controller
+   command which stops further IRQ-1 fires until BIOS re-enables on
+   the (never-coming) INT 16h drain.
+
+   Replacing the vector means BIOS INT 9 never runs, so the BIOS
+   buffer stays empty and the overflow beep never fires. The engine
+   doesn't read any BIOS keyboard state (shift flags at 0040:0017,
+   scan-to-ASCII translation, the 0040:001E buffer itself) -- it
+   consumes raw scan codes via SDL's scancode-to-keycode tables -- so
+   removing BIOS from the path has no engine-visible side effects.
+   Ctrl+Alt+Del system reset becomes unreachable mid-session, which is
+   acceptable for a port that owns the keyboard.
+
+   Implementation note: unlike the chain variant which internally
+   wraps the handler with IRET + register-save glue,
+   _go32_dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector expects a
+   pre-wrapped handler. We allocate the IRET wrapper explicitly via
+   _go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper, which is the matching
+   pre-allocated wrapper that CAN be freed with
+   _go32_dpmi_free_iret_wrapper on unhook (the chain wrapper cannot --
+   see the comment in DOS_UnhookInterrupt). The hook->replaced flag
+   tells DOS_UnhookInterrupt which path was used and whether the free
+   call is safe.
+
+   See docs/PHASE11-KEYBOARD-FINDINGS.md for the full mechanism
+   analysis and the pressure-test against all five observed real-HW
+   behaviors. */
+void DOS_HookInterruptReplace(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook)
+{
+    SDL_assert(irq >= 0 && irq <= 15);
+    SDL_assert(fn != NULL);
+    SDL_assert(hook != NULL);
+    hook->fn = fn;
+    hook->irq = irq;
+    hook->interrupt_vector = DOS_IRQToVector(irq);
+    hook->replaced = true;
+    hook->irq_handler_seginfo.pm_selector = _go32_my_cs();
+    hook->irq_handler_seginfo.pm_offset = (uint32_t)fn;
+    _go32_dpmi_get_protected_mode_interrupt_vector(hook->interrupt_vector, &hook->original_irq_handler_seginfo);
+
+    // Wrap our raw C function with an IRET + register-save trampoline.
+    // The trampoline writes its DPMI-allocated entry-point selector +
+    // offset back into hook->irq_handler_seginfo (overwriting the
+    // pm_selector / pm_offset we just set, which only fed the wrapper
+    // allocator with the target function); we then install the
+    // trampoline at the IRQ-1 vector.
+    _go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper(&hook->irq_handler_seginfo);
+    _go32_dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector(hook->interrupt_vector, &hook->irq_handler_seginfo);
+
+    // enable interrupt on the correct PIC (same as chaining variant)
+    if (irq > 7) {
+        outportb(PIC2_DATA, inportb(PIC2_DATA) & ~(1 << (irq - 8))); // unmask on slave PIC
+        outportb(PIC1_DATA, inportb(PIC1_DATA) & ~(1 << 2));         // ensure cascade (IRQ2) is unmasked
+    } else {
+        outportb(PIC1_DATA, inportb(PIC1_DATA) & ~(1 << irq)); // unmask on master PIC
+    }
+}
+
 void DOS_UnhookInterrupt(DOS_InterruptHook *hook, bool disable_interrupt)
 {
     if (!hook || !hook->fn) {
@@ -128,13 +196,21 @@ void DOS_UnhookInterrupt(DOS_InterruptHook *hook, bool disable_interrupt)
 
     _go32_dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector(hook->interrupt_vector, &hook->original_irq_handler_seginfo);
 
-    // Note: _go32_dpmi_chain_protected_mode_interrupt_vector internally
+    // _go32_dpmi_chain_protected_mode_interrupt_vector internally
     // allocates a wrapper, but it is NOT safe to free it with
     // _go32_dpmi_free_iret_wrapper — that function expects a wrapper
     // allocated by _go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper, and calling it on
-    // a chain-allocated wrapper causes a page fault on exit.  The wrapper
-    // is a few bytes of conventional memory that the OS reclaims when the
-    // process terminates, so the leak is harmless.
+    // a chain-allocated wrapper causes a page fault on exit. The wrapper
+    // is a few bytes of conventional memory that the OS reclaims when
+    // the process terminates, so the leak is harmless.
+    //
+    // DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): for hooks installed via
+    // DOS_HookInterruptReplace, the wrapper IS one returned by
+    // _go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper, so freeing it is safe and
+    // matched. The hook->replaced flag distinguishes the two paths.
+    if (hook->replaced) {
+        _go32_dpmi_free_iret_wrapper(&hook->irq_handler_seginfo);
+    }
 
     SDL_zerop(hook);
 }
diff --git a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
index 11dc58d5e..5b7ff09da 100644
--- a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
+++ b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
@@ -142,10 +142,33 @@ typedef struct DOS_InterruptHook
     int interrupt_vector; // this is the _vector_ number, not the IRQ number!
     _go32_dpmi_seginfo irq_handler_seginfo;
     _go32_dpmi_seginfo original_irq_handler_seginfo;
-
+    /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): true if hook was installed via
+       DOS_HookInterruptReplace (which uses _go32_dpmi_allocate_iret_wrapper
+       and _go32_dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector); false if it was
+       installed via DOS_HookInterrupt (which uses
+       _go32_dpmi_chain_protected_mode_interrupt_vector -- that function
+       allocates its own wrapper internally that is NOT safe to free with
+       _go32_dpmi_free_iret_wrapper).
+       DOS_UnhookInterrupt branches on this to free the wrapper safely
+       when applicable. */
+    bool replaced;
 } DOS_InterruptHook;
 
 void DOS_HookInterrupt(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook); // `irq` is the IRQ number, not the interrupt vector number!
+
+/* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): variant of DOS_HookInterrupt that REPLACES
+   the previous handler instead of chaining to it. Use this for IRQ-1
+   (keyboard) only, where chaining causes the BIOS INT 9 handler to fill
+   the BIOS keyboard buffer at 0040:001E in parallel with our SDL ring.
+   Once that 16-entry BIOS buffer fills (~10-16 keystrokes), the BIOS PC
+   speaker beeps on overflow and (on AMI/Award BIOSes) issues an 8042
+   "disable keyboard" command which stops further IRQ-1 fires entirely.
+   Replacing rather than chaining keeps BIOS out of the loop entirely.
+   See docs/PHASE11-KEYBOARD-FINDINGS.md for the full mechanism + risk
+   audit. Audio (IRQ-5) and any other hook should keep using the chaining
+   variant -- only IRQ-1 has a BIOS handler with stateful side effects. */
+void DOS_HookInterruptReplace(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook);
+
 void DOS_UnhookInterrupt(DOS_InterruptHook *hook, bool disable_interrupt);
 
 // Ends C function definitions when using C++
diff --git a/src/video/dos/SDL_dosevents.c b/src/video/dos/SDL_dosevents.c
index b29e07c6d..1a6e3261b 100644
--- a/src/video/dos/SDL_dosevents.c
+++ b/src/video/dos/SDL_dosevents.c
@@ -476,7 +476,22 @@ void DOSVESA_InitKeyboard(SDL_VideoDevice *device)
     DOS_LockVariable(doskutsu_kbd_irq_vector_at_install);
     DOS_LockVariable(doskutsu_kbd_pic1_mask_at_install);
 
-    DOS_HookInterrupt(1, KeyboardIRQHandler, &data->keyboard_interrupt_hook);
+    /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): use DOS_HookInterruptReplace, not
+       DOS_HookInterrupt. Chaining the IRQ-1 hook would let CWSDPMI's
+       reflection-to-real-mode stub forward the IRQ to the real-mode
+       BIOS INT 9 handler in parallel with our handler, which fills the
+       BIOS keyboard buffer at 0040:001E. After ~10-16 keystrokes the
+       buffer overflows, BIOS issues a PC speaker beep, and AMI/Award
+       BIOSes also issue an 8042 "disable keyboard" command that stops
+       further IRQ-1 fires entirely. Replacing the vector keeps BIOS
+       INT 9 out of the loop. The engine consumes raw scancodes via
+       SDL's keymap and never reads BIOS keyboard state, so removing
+       BIOS from the path has no engine-visible side effects. See
+       docs/PHASE11-KEYBOARD-FINDINGS.md for the full mechanism
+       analysis. Audio and any other future hook should keep using the
+       chaining DOS_HookInterrupt; only IRQ-1 has a BIOS handler with
+       stateful side effects. */
+    DOS_HookInterruptReplace(1, KeyboardIRQHandler, &data->keyboard_interrupt_hook);
 
     /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 9 wave 13.6.2): post-hook install-time
        snapshots for the IRQ-mask discrimination iter. Ordering of the
